theory.intro:
A **quadratic model** uses a parabola to describe a real situation. The vertex gives the largest or smallest value, the intercepts show where the input or output is zero, and only part of the parabola makes sense in context.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-0.4,0) -- (6.4,0) node[right] {time};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-0.4) -- (0,4.4) node[above] {height};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,domain=0:5.458,samples=100,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.9+1.8*\x-0.36*\x*\x});
\fill (0,0.9) circle (2pt);
\fill (2.5,3.15) circle (2pt);
\fill (5.458,0) circle (2pt);
\node[right] at (2.65,3.35) {vertex: greatest height};
\node[right] at (0.2,0.5) {starting height};
\node[below] at (5.458,-0.15) {lands};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
A ball is thrown upwards from a platform. Its height $h$ metres after $t$ seconds is modelled by $h=-t^{2}+6t+7$. The full parabola is shown.
Find:
(a) the initial height;
(b) the maximum height and when it occurs;
(c) when the ball reaches the ground;
(d) the two times when the ball is $12$ m high;
(e) the height after $2$ seconds, using the equation.
State the time interval for which the model describes the flight.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{modelblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black,->},xmin=-2,xmax=8,ymin=-10,ymax=18,xtick={-2,-1,...,8},ytick={-8,-4,0,4,8,12,16},grid=major,grid style={draw=black!20},scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$t$ (seconds)},ylabel={$h$ (metres)},clip=true]
\addplot[modelblue,thick,domain=-2:8,samples=101] {-x*x+6*x+7};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
(a) The vertical intercept is $(0,7)$, so the ball starts $7$ m above the ground.
(b) The vertex is $(3,16)$. The maximum height is $16$ m, reached after $3$ seconds.
(c) The horizontal intercepts are $t=-1$ and $t=7$. Negative time is excluded, so the ball reaches the ground after $7$ seconds.
(d) A horizontal line at $h=12$ meets the curve at $t=1$ and $t=5$. The ball is $12$ m high after $1$ second and after $5$ seconds.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{modelblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=7cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={black,->},xmin=-2,xmax=8,ymin=-10,ymax=18,xtick={-2,-1,...,8},ytick={-8,-4,0,4,8,12,16},grid=major,grid style={draw=black!20},scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$t$ (seconds)},ylabel={$h$ (metres)},clip=true]
\addplot[modelblue,thick,domain=-2:8,samples=101] {-x*x+6*x+7};
\draw[black,dashed] (axis cs:0,12)--(axis cs:5,12);
\draw[black,dashed] (axis cs:1,0)--(axis cs:1,12);
\draw[black,dashed] (axis cs:5,0)--(axis cs:5,12);
\addplot[only marks,mark=*,black] coordinates {(0,7) (3,16) (7,0) (1,12) (5,12)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(e) For $t=2$:
$\begin{aligned}h&=-(2)^{2}+6(2)+7\\&=-4+12+7\\&=15\end{aligned}$
The height is $15$ m.
The flight is modelled for $0\le t\le7$. Negative times precede the throw; after $7$ seconds the equation gives heights below ground.

workedExamples.question_text:
During the first downward movement of a bungee jump, the jumper's height $h$ metres above the ground after $t$ seconds is modelled by $h=2t^{2}-12t+23$. The graph shows $0\le t\le3$.
Use the graph to find the starting height, the lowest height, and when the jumper reaches that lowest height.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{modelblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=6cm,axis lines=left,axis line style={black,->},xmin=0,xmax=3.5,ymin=0,ymax=25,xtick={0,0.5,...,3},ytick={0,5,...,25},grid=major,grid style={draw=black!20},scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},xlabel={$t$ (seconds)},ylabel={$h$ (metres)}]
\addplot[modelblue,thick,domain=0:3,samples=81] {2*x*x-12*x+23};
